pr_comments:
@gpshead I'd like to get this PR past the finish line, but I am not familiar enough with zip format intricacies to follow-up on your last comment, so I'd appreciate it if you could provide additional guidance!
sounds like the best approach might be to add more test cases that cover the security-oriented cases you're concerned about? if you could propose such test cases, or provide pointers that could help me with coming up with such test cases, this might move us forward!

fwiw, I just merged main into this branch locally and it merges cleanly and the test suite still passes, so we should be in a good spot to pick this up.

@gpshead As an example, both `zipfile` and `zipimport` are not safe against (for example) zip bombs today.  My goal in writing it this way was to take valid zips that did not work (at all) before, and make them work like they do in zipfile, so if anything I'm aiming for _parity_ as a goal.

I hope that the security-focused aspect, which is quite possibly a breaking change for real-world files _that work today_, can be handled in another issue -- quite likely a PEP, and actually unifying the code for zipfile and zipimport.  Both libraries handle files that do not conform to what I would consider "zip: the good parts" ([ISO/IEC 21320-1:2015](https://www.iso.org/standard/60101.html) is a start), and I'm almost certain I can construct non-zip64 zip files that are already handled differently between zipfile and zipimport.  There's only a _very_ restrictive subset that is handled universally among decompressors without bugs, and we'd need to have a much larger discussion about what features to prohibit.

Even decompressors that have been around for ages (like Info-Zip) have behavior differences that I would call bugs -- either accepting invalid files or rejecting valid-to-the-appnote ones.  Sorry this is so complicated.
